  "summary": "Today's health-tech landscape shows tension between innovation incentives and healthcare access alongside significant industry consolidation. A California Supreme Court ruling favored Gilead in a \"duty to innovate\" case, while Medicare is simultaneously tightening pathways for breakthrough devices to access extra payments, signaling stricter scrutiny of expedited reimbursement. The sector saw notable M&A activity including a Supernus-Indivior merger to create a CNS-focused company and reports of potential pharma mega-deals involving AstraZeneca and Bristol Myers Squibb, though investor concerns about valuation are emerging. On the access front, rural telehealth adoption continues lagging urban areas across chronic conditions, and a major health system-insurer contract dispute in Virginia threatens coverage for 380,000 people. Meanwhile, AI implementation in healthcare is moving forward unevenly—hospitals struggle to sustain AI projects despite promising applications in disease surveillance and diagnostics, while concerns mount about AI scribes affecting clinical education. Funding remained robust across digital health and biotech, with notable raises for genome sequencing and AI-powered diagnostic tools.",
